Projects must make European clean-energy supply chains less dependent on critical raw materials and more diverse, while delivering measurable gains for the challenge owner.
The call text asks every project to:
- reduce dependency on critical materials and supply chain weak points in wind, solar and grid technologies;
- support the green and digital transition;
- explicitly show alignment with the Critical Raw Materials Act and the Net-Zero Industry Act, for example through CO2 or resource savings or moving part of a value chain into the EU. The Net-Zero Industry Act aims for at least 40% of net-zero technology manufacturing, including grid technologies, to take place in the EU.
The challenges add their own targets: safer offshore maintenance without rope access (Challenge 1), lower cost and shorter lead times for suction anchors (Challenge 2), longer battery life and less waste (Challenge 3), and fewer delays and safer offshore installation during satellite-navigation outages (Challenge 4). Each funded SME must report measured or well-justified estimated results and the related earnings or savings in its final deliverable.

The solution must be new or clearly improved, but it may build on existing technology; what counts is a working answer to the challenge owner's problem.
The call scores the level of innovation and ambition, including the novelty and added value of the solution. The challenges ask for applied, solution-oriented proposals that may combine hardware, software, data services or integrated solutions. The innovation can lie in:
- technology and products, such as robots, sensors, positioning systems or battery diagnostics;
- manufacturing processes, such as automated welding or digital quality control;
- business models, which Challenge 3 explicitly requires for second-life batteries.
Examples of eligible innovations in the call text include substitutes for critical materials, new suppliers validated through pilot tests, and digital tools for supply chain monitoring and traceability.
